From: Roman Rikhter <teledurak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw89: pci: kick the TX queues on TX completion

When a TX queue holds more frames than the DMA ring has room for,
rtw89_core_txq_schedule() sends what fits and runs again from a delayed
work one jiffy later. Nothing else restarts it: the TX queue work runs
from wake_tx_queue, from that delayed work and at the end of a
remain-on-channel. After a burst that filled the ring, the rest waits for
the timer (up to 10 ms at HZ=100), even if the ring has long drained.

Queue the TX queue work whenever the release report queue returned TX
buffers, as other drivers do on TX completion (mt76 on TX free events,
ath10k from its completion NAPI). The delayed work stays as a fallback.

Signed-off-by: Roman Rikhter <teledurak@gmail.com>
---
diff --git drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw89/pci.c drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw89/pci.c
index 4a26b5b..bdfdc69 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw89/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw89/pci.c
@@ -847,6 +847,10 @@ static int rtw89_pci_poll_rpq_dma(struct rtw89_dev *rtwdev,
 out_unlock:
 	spin_unlock_bh(&rtwpci->trx_lock);
 
+	/* frames left in the TX queues may wait for what was released */
+	if (cnt)
+		queue_work(rtwdev->txq_wq, &rtwdev->txq_work);
+
 	/* always release all RPQ */
 	work_done = min_t(int, cnt, budget);
 	rtwdev->napi_budget_countdown -= work_done;
